.TH CCOL_DYNMQ_ENABLE_SENDING 3 "2026-09-20" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
ccol_dynmq_enable_sending \- turn sends on again on a dynamic queue
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cthreadcomm.h>

ccol_retval_t ccol_dynmq_enable_sending(ccol_dynamic_queue *dq);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
This function clears the flag that ccol_dynmq_disable_sending() set, so
the queue accepts a send again and does not return ccol_not_permitted.
A new queue has sends turned on from the start, so you need this call
only after a call turned sends off.

The function wakes every write-direction waiter on this queue, which is
a ccol_select() call, a ccol_select_timed() call, or an ccol_event_loop
registration, so a waiter that parked while sends were off sees that
the queue is writable again.

The messages in the queue from before the change do not change: a turn
off and a turn on never drop anything.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
dq   Dynamic queue that gets sends turned on
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
ccol_success       - The function turned sends on
ccol_invalid_args  - dq is NULL
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
A new queue has sends turned on from the start.
The function is thread-safe.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR ccol_dynmq_disable_sending (3),
.BR ccol_dynmq_send_zc (3),
.BR ccol_dynmq_msg_count (3),
.BR ccol_circq_enable_sending (3)
